  "cues": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 2.180000066757202,
      "text": "Begin with the nephron overview.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 3.0199999809265137,
      "end": 8.65999984741211,
      "text": "A is the renal corpuscle, B the proximal convoluted tubule, C the descending",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 8.65999984741211,
      "end": 11.619999885559082,
      "text": "limb and D the ascending limb of the nephron loop.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 12.34000015258789,
      "end": 16.5,
      "text": "E is the distal convoluted tubule, leading toward the collecting duct at F.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 17.399999618530273,
      "end": 22.18000030517578,
      "text": "Follow the arrows down C, around the hairpin and up D before reaching E.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 22.81999969482422,
      "end": 26.420000076293945,
      "text": "The collecting duct receives fluid from more than one nephron and is included",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 26.420000076293945,
      "end": 27.15999984741211,
      "text": "for continuity.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 27.65999984741211,
      "end": 31.84000015258789,
      "text": "The dashed line separates cortex from medulla in this simplified view.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 32.7599983215332,
      "end": 36.47999954223633,
      "text": "Loop depth varies among nephron types, and this drawing is not a scale",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 36.47999954223633,
      "end": 37.400001525878906,
      "text": "measurement of a kidney.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 37.96800000000055,
      "end": 42.62799984741266,
      "text": "The overview shows a fluid route, not a claim that everything entering it must",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 42.62799984741266,
      "end": 43.40800005722101,
      "text": "leave as urine.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 44.268000190735414,
      "end": 47.867999618530824,
      "text": "Reabsorption returns material from tubular fluid toward the circulation;",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 48.8279996566778,
      "end": 52.90594882603236,
      "text": "secretion adds selected material from the blood or interstitial side to",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 52.90594882603236,
      "end": 53.30800015258844,
      "text": "tubular",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 53.30800015258844,
      "end": 53.64800030517633,
      "text": "fluid.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 54.24800068664606,
      "end": 58.805692774259676,
      "text": "The proximal tubule at B is an important site of recovery of filtered water",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 58.805692774259676,
      "end": 58.98800045776422,
      "text": "and",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 58.98800045776422,
      "end": 59.30800015258844,
      "text": "solutes.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 59.98800045776422,
      "end": 64.46800000000056,
      "text": "In the standard loop comparison, the descending limb at C permits water",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 64.46800000000056,
      "end": 69.607999389649,
      "text": "movement, whereas the ascending limb at D reabsorbs salts and is relatively",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 69.607999389649,
      "end": 70.80800015258845,
      "text": "impermeable to water.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 71.5080009155279,
      "end": 74.02800137329157,
      "text": "These differences contribute to concentrating mechanisms.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 74.80800015258845,
      "end": 78.92799908447321,
      "text": "Do not swap blood flow arrows with tubular flow arrows or interpret filtered",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 78.92799908447321,
      "end": 81.72799832153376,
      "text": "glucose as automatically excreted glucose.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 82.12800000000134,
      "end": 84.7279999046339,
      "text": "Switch to the renal corpuscle close up.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 85.46799991417065,
      "end": 90.56799958038464,
      "text": "The solid arrows identify blood flow: afferent arteriole A enters the",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 90.56799958038464,
      "end": 95.66799996185436,
      "text": "glomerular capillary tuft B, and efferent arteriole D carries blood away.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 96.20799992370739,
      "end": 100.08799908447399,
      "text": "The dashed arrows show a different route: filtration from the tuft toward",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 100.08799908447399,
      "end": 104.46800015258923,
      "text": "capsular space C and onward toward the proximal tubule at E.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 104.88800022888317,
      "end": 107.08799908447399,
      "text": "E is not the efferent arteriole.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 107.08799908447399,
      "end": 111.62800000000134,
      "text": "Two exit routes appear because blood and filtrate are different compartments.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 112.32800076294079,
      "end": 116.86800167846813,
      "text": "The sketch cuts the capsule open schematically at its vascular and tubular",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 116.86800167846813,
      "end": 120.32800076294079,
      "text": "poles; it does not show every layer of the filtration barrier.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 121.20800183105602,
      "end": 123.12800000000134,
      "text": "Read the arrow key before naming an outlet.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 123.48000000000201,
      "end": 128.15999982834063,
      "text": "The normal glomerular filtration barrier retains blood cells and most large",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 128.15999982834063,
      "end": 132.44000003814898,
      "text": "plasma proteins while allowing water and many small solutes to enter the",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 132.44000003814898,
      "end": 133.2599997329732,
      "text": "capsular space.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 134.37999961853228,
      "end": 137.9199995803853,
      "text": "Filtrate therefore contains useful substances as well as waste products.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 138.74000022888384,
      "end": 141.92000053405962,
      "text": "The subsequent tubules recover much of the useful material.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 142.71999977112017,
      "end": 146.706923820302,
      "text": "Do not describe the glomerulus as a sieve that selectively removes only",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 146.706923820302,
      "end": 147.1000008392354,
      "text": "wastes.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 148.11999938965045,
      "end": 151.7700008177166,
      "text": "Likewise, the size of a drawn tuft does not provide a measured filtration",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 151.7700008177166,
      "end": 152.02000091552935,
      "text": "rate.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 152.87999961853228,
      "end": 155.61999938965045,
      "text": "Calculating a rate requires data beyond this schematic.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 156.75999877929888,
      "end": 160.54000137329302,
      "text": "Keep the normal barrier, the composition of the initial filtrate and later",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 160.54000137329302,
      "end": 163.5799984741231,
      "text": "tubular processing as three distinct parts of the explanation.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 163.99200000000266,
      "end": 167.7120000286129,
      "text": "Now identify a typical myelinated multipolar neuron.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 168.65199984741477,
      "end": 173.3250773573682,
      "text": "A points to branching dendrites and B to the cell body, or soma, containing",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 173.3250773573682,
      "end": 173.51200045776633,
      "text": "the",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 173.51200045776633,
      "end": 173.83200015259055,
      "text": "nucleus.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 174.37200011444358,
      "end": 177.05200041961936,
      "text": "C identifies the axon near its origin.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 178.0319999618557,
      "end": 183.7919992370632,
      "text": "D marks a myelin segment, E a node of Ranvier between segments, and F the",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 183.7919992370632,
      "end": 184.83200015259055,
      "text": "branching axon terminals.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 185.37199916076926,
      "end": 188.81199969482688,
      "text": "The lower arrow indicates the usual functional route toward the terminals.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 189.55199946594504,
      "end": 193.45199908447532,
      "text": "Do not identify dendrites only by the left side of a page: another figure can",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 193.45199908447532,
      "end": 194.01200045776633,
      "text": "be rotated.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 194.93200053406028,
      "end": 198.43200019171564,
      "text": "Neuron shapes vary, so this schematic is not a universal shape for all",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 198.43200019171564,
      "end": 198.83200015259055,
      "text": "sensory,",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 199.23200167846946,
      "end": 200.93199862671165,
      "text": "motor and interneuron classes.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 201.6319993896511,
      "end": 204.51200045776633,
      "text": "Use branching, continuity and the label endpoints together.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 205.15200000000334,
      "end": 208.37200002861357,
      "text": "Keep the neuron overview and compare D with E.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 209.31199984741545,
      "end": 213.7319999237094,
      "text": "Myelin around the axon at D acts as insulation that reduces current leakage.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 214.41200022888518,
      "end": 218.59199958038664,
      "text": "In the standard myelinated axon account, local electrical current spreads",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 218.59199958038664,
      "end": 223.3663591122783,
      "text": "between nodes and action potentials are regenerated at nodal regions such as",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 223.3663591122783,
      "end": 223.49200015259123,
      "text": "E.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 223.97199969482756,
      "end": 225.9519992370639,
      "text": "This is called saltatory conduction.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 226.672000457767,
      "end": 230.43200068664885,
      "text": "The signal does not mean that one sodium ion physically jumps from the cell",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 230.43200068664885,
      "end": 235.13199954223967,
      "text": "body to every terminal, and it is not a transmitter filled vesicle travelling",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 235.13199954223967,
      "end": 236.41200022888518,
      "text": "as the action potential.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 237.47199969482756,
      "end": 240.09199862671232,
      "text": "Myelin thickness and node spacing are not drawn to scale.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 241.05200152588225,
      "end": 245.41199832153654,
      "text": "Identify the sheath, axon and node separately before connecting each to its",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 245.41199832153654,
      "end": 245.73200183105803,
      "text": "function.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 246.000000000004,
      "end": 249.53999996185703,
      "text": "The synapse close up enlarges a typical chemical junction.",
      "chapter": 6
    },
    {
      "start": 250.30000019073887,
      "end": 253.03999996185703,
      "text": "A identifies a vesicle inside the presynaptic terminal.",
      "chapter": 6
    },
    {
      "start": 253.76000022888584,
      "end": 259.1999998092691,
      "text": "B points to the presynaptic membrane, C the synaptic cleft, and D receptors at",
      "chapter": 6
    },
    {
      "start": 259.1999998092691,
      "end": 260.34000015259187,
      "text": "the postsynaptic membrane.",
      "chapter": 6
    },
    {
      "start": 261.2200002670328,
      "end": 265.2999992370645,
      "text": "The small dots represent released transmitter molecules, not whole vesicles",
      "chapter": 6
    },
    {
      "start": 265.2999992370645,
      "end": 266.3199996948282,
      "text": "crossing the gap.",
      "chapter": 6
    },
    {
      "start": 266.7800006866495,
      "end": 270.500000000004,
      "text": "A transmitter must reach suitable receptors to influence the receiving cell.",
      "chapter": 6
    },
    {
      "start": 270.8799991607706,
      "end": 275.2800006866495,
      "text": "The membranes remain distinct across the cleft; they are not drawn as one",
      "chapter": 6
    },
    {
      "start": 275.2800006866495,
      "end": 276.3199996948282,
      "text": "continuous axon.",
      "chapter": 6
    },
    {
      "start": 277.1399993896524,
      "end": 279.7200012207071,
      "text": "Read the upper and lower cell labels as well as the arrows.",
      "chapter": 6
    },
    {
      "start": 280.45999908447664,
      "end": 284.1661520737908,
      "text": "This figure shows a chemical synapse and should not be used as a complete",
      "chapter": 6
    },
    {
      "start": 284.1661520737908,
      "end": 284.4199981689493,
      "text": "model",
      "chapter": 6
    },
    {
      "start": 284.4199981689493,
      "end": 285.58000183105867,
      "text": "of an electrical synapse.",
      "chapter": 6
    },
    {
      "start": 286.65600000000467,
      "end": 290.936000209813,
      "text": "In the typical chemical synapse account, arrival of an action potential at the",
      "chapter": 7
    },
    {
      "start": 290.936000209813,
      "end": 295.89599977112283,
      "text": "presynaptic terminal leads to calcium entry and transmitter release by vesicle",
      "chapter": 7
    },
    {
      "start": 295.89599977112283,
      "end": 296.2560003814744,
      "text": "fusion.",
      "chapter": 7
    },
    {
      "start": 297.4160002288865,
      "end": 301.89599977112283,
      "text": "Released molecules cross the cleft at C and act on appropriate receptors at D.",
      "chapter": 7
    },
    {
      "start": 302.61600003815164,
      "end": 307.27600083923807,
      "text": "The resulting response can be excitatory or inhibitory, depending on the",
      "chapter": 7
    },
    {
      "start": 307.27600083923807,
      "end": 311.27600083923807,
      "text": "receptor and cellular context, and does not guarantee a new action potential",
      "chapter": 7
    },
    {
      "start": 311.27600083923807,
      "end": 312.7560003814744,
      "text": "after every release event.",
      "chapter": 7
    },
    {
      "start": 313.51600061035623,
      "end": 317.1159990844773,
      "text": "Direction in this diagram follows the asymmetric arrangement of release",
      "chapter": 7
    },
    {
      "start": 317.1159990844773,
      "end": 318.37599931335916,
      "text": "machinery and receptors.",
      "chapter": 7
    },
    {
      "start": 319.2959993896531,
      "end": 322.635999542241,
      "text": "Do not describe the gap as a place where an intact action potential simply",
      "chapter": 7
    },
    {
      "start": 322.635999542241,
      "end": 323.83600030518045,
      "text": "leaps through open space.",
      "chapter": 7
    },
    {
      "start": 324.83600030518045,
      "end": 328.2959993896531,
      "text": "Electrical synapses use a different coupling mechanism that is not pictured",
      "chapter": 7
    },
    {
      "start": 328.2959993896531,
      "end": 328.65600000000467,
      "text": "here.",
      "chapter": 7
    }
  ]
}
